  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Wisconsin for FY2026 to date total $3B, recorded across 54,063 awards with an average award size of $56.2K. Obligations fell 51.5% year over year. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, obligating $2.1B in the current fiscal year to date, followed by the Department of Defense with $574M and the Department of Agriculture with $179M. OPTUMSERVE HEALTH SERVICES, INC. is the largest vendor, receiving $1.9B in obligations. Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) is the largest industry by obligations at $1.8B, followed by Truck Trailer Manufacturing at $365M and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $291M. Historical context shows prior-year obligations of $8.9B in FY24 and $6.3B in FY25, with earlier totals of $5.8B in FY21, $5.2B in FY22, and $5.3B in FY23. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How big is Wisconsin’s federal contracting market right now?",
        "body": "The state has $3B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 51.5% year over year and the award count stands at 54,063 with an average award size of $56.2K. Recent fiscal years show larger totals, including $8.9B in FY24 and $6.3B in FY25."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate contracting in Wisconsin?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the top buyer with $2.1B in obligations, followed by the Department of Defense with $574M and the Department of Agriculture with $179M. Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) lead industries with $1.8B, while Truck Trailer Manufacturing has $365M and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing has $291M. OPTUMSERVE HEALTH SERVICES, INC. is the top vendor with $1.9B in obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
